  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe problem seeks to minimize C\u2085 via an optimal step function h while respecting constraints. The current gradient descent approach may be limited by local optima and slow convergence. We propose an Evolutionary Strategy (ES) to explore the solution space more effectively.\n\nThe key idea is to maintain a population of candidate solutions, applying mutation and selection to iteratively improve them. This approach leverages global optimization principles, escaping local minima and potentially finding superior solutions.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^(n_points), sum(h) = n_points / 2\n- Objective: min C\u2085 = max_k [sum(h[i](1 - h[i + k])) * dx]\n- Mutation: Add normal noise to selected candidates\n- Selection: Keep top N candidates based on C\u2085\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n1. Initialize population with `initial_h_values` and random valid h's\n2. Evaluate fitness (C\u2085) for all candidates\n3. While time remains:\n   a. Select top 20% candidates\n   b. Mutate each by adding normal noise\n   c. Clip to [0,1] and renormalize sum\n   d. Evaluate new candidates\n   e. Replace worst old candidates with new ones\n4. Return best h found\n\n4. Search choices\n- Population size: 50\n- Mutation rate: 0.1\n- Selection ratio: 0.2\n- Initial: 10% initial_h_based, 90% random valid\n\n5. Compute budget\n- Mutation and selection are O(N), evaluation O(N^2)\n- With N=50, iterations feasible within 1100s\n\n6. Validation\nEnsure h \u2208 [0,1] and sum(h)=n_points/2 at all steps",
